#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079547} :activity/id #uuid "92a67e2b-bdab-5391-a359-7c186fb75ce2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048202 :topic/id #uuid "87acae7d-39e1-5216-acf9-966560dccc34" :topic/math-academy-id 650} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079672 :tutorial/content "A differential operator $D$ is an operator that acts on a function $y(x)$ by forming an expression involving $y$ and one or more of its derivatives.\n\nFor example, consider the following differential operator.\n\n$$\nD(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}+2\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + y\n$$\n\nThis is a **second-order differential operator** because the highest derivative is of order $2.$ \n\nWe can apply $D$ to any function $y(x)$ that's twice-differentiable. For example, let's apply the operator to $y(x) = \\sin x.$ We substitute this function into the operator and calculate the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(\\operatorname{sin}x) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x)+\\operatorname{sin}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x)+2\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x+2\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can think of our operator $D$ as a transformation that takes a *function* $y(x)$ as input and produces *another function* as output. In this example, the operator $D$ took the function $\\sin x$ and mapped it to the function $2\\cos x$:\n\n$$\nD: \\sin x \\longrightarrow 2\\cos x\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to draw a parallel with a **linear transformation** in vector spaces, \n\n$$\nT: \\mathbf v \\longrightarrow \\mathbf w\n$$\n\nwhich takes a vector $\\mathbf v$ as input and produces another vector $\\mathbf w$ as output. The analogies between operators on function spaces and transformations on vector spaces will become clearer as we progress." :tutorial/id #uuid "c46ae630-9618-5c5f-951e-b66234fab409" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4085 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b744a42f-230c-5f20-bb57-d8014dc80f0d" :step/math-academy-id 593341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079614 :tutorial/content "In the last example, we defined a *second-order differential operator* as\n\n$$\nD(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}+2\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + y.\n$$\n\nUnder this notation, it is assumed that the function $y(x)$ is passed as input to the operator. \n\nWe sometimes prefer not to specify which function is being used as input. In that case, we may write the operator itself as\n\n$$\nD=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}}{\\textrm d x^{2}}+2\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x} + 1.\n$$\n\nUsing this notation, we can apply the operator to any suitable function. So, if $f(x)$ is a twice-differentiable function, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(f) & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+1)f \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}f}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}+f.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThink of this as *applying* the operator $D$ *to* the function $f.$\n\nFor the first part of this lesson, we'll restrict our attention to differential operators that are of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = a_n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + a_{n-1}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + a_{1}\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + a_0y\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0, a_1, \\ldots, a_n$ are all constants. We'll encounter more general forms later in the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "d00f8f83-f07f-5df7-8695-629e5f2a3130" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14619 :tutorial/title "Notation"} :step/id #uuid "ca935020-b364-5a10-b8ff-b9f4d6c659c6" :step/math-academy-id 593342 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078588 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078335 :question/id #uuid "91e7f8e9-dc03-5203-9d33-0175960ba276" :question/math-academy-id "e-6570"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3823db-6df8-502a-a050-b5aa169d2e9d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368209}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Constant Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "8353cd5b-82c6-547b-8d15-2f52b7c49334" :step/math-academy-id 593343 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079550}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079613 :tutorial/content "Differential operators can be quite complex, involving products of the function $y(x)$, its derivatives, and the independent variable $x$.\n\nFor example, consider the following **third-order differential operator**:\n\n$$\nD(y)=y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + x^2y\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + xy\n$$\n\nLet's apply this operator to the function $y(x) = x^2$. \n\n**Step 1:** Substitute $y=x^2$ into the operator expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & ={y}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{x}^{2}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy \\\\  & =({x}^{2})^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({x}^{2})+{x}^{2}({x}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+x({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({x}^{2})+{x}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Calculate the required derivatives step-by-step.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & ={x}^{4}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(2x)+{x}^{4}(2x)+{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2)+2{x}^{5}+{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\cdot (0)+2{x}^{5}+{x}^{3} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}+{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we'll give the general definition of the $n$-th order differential operator and explain the difference between linear and non-linear differential operators." :tutorial/id #uuid "cd93b2d8-0c4f-53d5-b4e9-770da3bea5d4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14618 :tutorial/title "More Complex Differential Operators"} :step/id #uuid "74f88a92-ab79-5a2a-8101-1a336fba27bd" :step/math-academy-id 593346 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079551 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078589 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078336 :question/id #uuid "0fba12b1-fa78-5ff2-88e4-2a690105d321" :question/math-academy-id "e-6571"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ff7f935-dcec-5b4c-ba1d-e0b4c9e197a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420955}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Functions of X as Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "839312a2-5864-5b67-87cc-32bb715dfbb7" :step/math-academy-id 593347 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079552}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078590 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078215 :question/id #uuid "31a9b20e-d159-5ff2-94dc-54b8d7b8e620" :question/math-academy-id "e-21785"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7066d712-f0a3-554f-9d10-3d6fe4b95a18" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420954}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Multivariable Functions as Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "ceef53f4-191f-5406-bbe5-2e4c0a833802" :step/math-academy-id 593350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079618 :tutorial/content "The properties of a differential operator depend on its structure. A key distinction is whether an operator is **linear** or **non-linear**.\n\nA general **$n$-th order linear differential operator** has the form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y,\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are functions of the independent variable $x$ only.\n\nFor example, the operator\n\n$$\nD(y) = x y''' - y\n$$\n\nis a *linear operator*, since its coefficients ($x$ and $-1$) depend only on $x$.\n\nIn contrast, the operator from our previous example,\n\n$$\nD(y)=y^2 y''' + x^2y y' + xy,\n$$\n\n is *non-linear* because some of its coefficients depend on $y$. For instance, the coefficient of $y'''$ is $y^2$.\n\nOther operators are non-linear because they involve powers of derivatives or other non-linear functions of $y$. For example,\n\n$$\nD(y) = \\left(y' \\right)^2 + y\n$$\n\nis non-linear due to the $(y')^2$ term.\n\nFor the rest of this topic, we will focus primarily on linear operators, as non-linear cases are typically more complex." :tutorial/id #uuid "b85dce79-a68a-5df3-b681-6a996c10ca48" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14903 :tutorial/title "A General n-th Order Linear Differential Operator."} :step/id #uuid "ccac473f-1a73-5b81-8a15-00ee17de748f" :step/math-academy-id 608079 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079551}}] :activity/title "Differential Operators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078672}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420957} :activity/id #uuid "d94bb670-9ae3-5e3f-a2c8-33c1515f220f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048202 :topic/id #uuid "87acae7d-39e1-5216-acf9-966560dccc34" :topic/math-academy-id 650} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420956 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ae9dacd-9221-5bef-a3e2-054140ce493a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363040} {:answer/id #uuid "9d49b809-9260-5d2e-bf3c-edbf1cfbe326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363041} {:answer/id #uuid "311f4edb-1ed9-5bdd-a81b-ea812b499a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363042} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf0b43a-7db8-50c5-a40c-5a36e706ef99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363043} {:answer/id #uuid "857a50bb-2584-5d08-9f49-c2a69907123b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363044}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363040} :answer-field/id #uuid "438741cb-1bf1-5078-92c8-7fcd9f079cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a136203-dd8c-5077-8693-b3c3a213b678" :question/math-academy-id "q-130248" :question/problem "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y.\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}={x}^{2},{f}_{2}=3x,{f}_{1}=-1,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{x})+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+3x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+3x(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2788ab1a-6e0a-5a50-87b3-604347a0ca06" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420960}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420957 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49f46a15-0ca3-5841-b2b5-75e0e85c81df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345982} {:answer/id #uuid "60b84d6c-d8f3-52d7-8796-88e3110d630d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346189} {:answer/id #uuid "adbb7b5a-753f-5d50-9dee-99ba55ca3aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346866} {:answer/id #uuid "b2aa1892-d783-5d98-8e84-8e8aa43ec658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346908} {:answer/id #uuid "b54dbd62-5d7e-5ae5-8499-c4d6f981662d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346930}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346908} :answer-field/id #uuid "e68a9f18-b7c7-5562-af22-77d1084739c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e13f24b1-d2d1-59a7-beb7-3c206203362c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79607" :question/problem "Given that $D=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=-1,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =(x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8241c705-b9e7-57f5-b848-6f01dcbd8104" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420956}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420958 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148704c0-66d6-5a7a-bcfa-a356fdfee3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420942} {:answer/id #uuid "a151a26f-0185-5b72-ad6e-e8d97e752462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420947} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0872b3-dcd3-51dc-9d2f-3beeca91ae9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420948} {:answer/id #uuid "ae217ed4-c1eb-595c-8665-669047155b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420949} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fc8ffc-6f90-5b2d-89f4-fba4f7227db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420951}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420951} :answer-field/id #uuid "8afc7adc-5a21-5274-a5f6-0ea02bdd2cb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fbf85b19-bc66-596e-8aa5-463e28e677ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-337842" :question/problem "Given that $D=3{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=x-5?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=3{x}^{2},{f}_{1}=-x,$ and ${f}_{0}=-2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(x-5) \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2)(x-5) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(x-5)-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-5)-2(x-5) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot 0-x\\cdot 1-2x+10 \\\\  & =10-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a436a1a5-5fb0-5e0f-bd7c-73fb351028a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420959}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420959 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222969f9-58f5-5eaf-a95c-a222814267da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7x(3-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420943} {:answer/id #uuid "8bac3d9f-afab-54c5-9178-6a62e26c0328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7x(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420944} {:answer/id #uuid "99683a50-a2e0-5574-b392-ab97ccf08e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7x(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420945} {:answer/id #uuid "9976156a-3f38-595e-80df-31172f261865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(7x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420946} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f9b738-9d10-511a-bb0d-c26b4c5ea3fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7x(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420945} :answer-field/id #uuid "ada71037-9568-53ad-8084-566ff6d93eeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "013a6ab9-35f4-5a21-b21c-f8fd4e726466" :question/math-academy-id "q-337849" :question/problem "If $D(y)=xy\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y,$ then $D({x}^{2}-3x)=$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=xy,{f}_{1}=-y,$ and ${f}_{0}=4.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={x}^{2}-3x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({x}^{2}-3x) & =x({x}^{2}-3x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}-3x)-({x}^{2}-3x)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3x)+4({x}^{2}-3x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-3x)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-3)-({x}^{2}-3x)(2x-3)+4({x}^{2}-3x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-3x)\\cdot 2-({x}^{2}-3x)(2x-3)+4({x}^{2}-3x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+6{x}^{2}-9x+4{x}^{2}-12x \\\\  & =7{x}^{2}-21x \\\\  & =7x(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b3253494-9414-583a-9498-13ee9c127821"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420960 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e5e6c5-ab44-55be-aa90-70e3ce45de4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{4x}(3-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308749} {:answer/id #uuid "532ca6a0-76bf-59b9-ac93-ed25573ae8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{4x}(2-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309379} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c61d17-3bb6-5ef5-910a-22a07c3083bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{4x}(3+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310110} {:answer/id #uuid "bfaa76f0-ebc0-54cd-9a74-7c0e1bc19d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{4x}(1+3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310294} {:answer/id #uuid "f828d022-1ad4-577a-a879-55bc73919c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{4x}(2+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310724}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308749} :answer-field/id #uuid "faf11ab5-29be-5943-bd83-83fafb691233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3aca8975-6bdc-5d74-9fd7-116773d083f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-337559" :question/problem "If $D(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2},$ then $D({e}^{2x})=$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=y,{f}_{1}=-xy,$ and ${f}_{0}=2y.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y={e}^{2x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{2x})-x({e}^{2x})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x})+2({e}^{2x})^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{2x})-x{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}+2{e}^{4x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 4{e}^{2x}-2x{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =4{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =6{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4x}(3-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e766b3ed-4c61-58c4-87d1-bc09d539d77b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420958}}] :activity/title "Differential Operators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420941}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120572} :activity/id #uuid "a165e965-4bdc-5070-bd4a-beeb5a8e6ae9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048418 :topic/id #uuid "9d4da633-779f-5c01-bae4-e1784b64c369" :topic/math-academy-id 1837} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120999 :tutorial/content "For a curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=x(t), \\quad y=y(t), \\qquad t\\in [a,b],\n$$\n\nthe arc length of the curve between $t=a$ and $t=b$ is given by\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow, if we express our curve as a vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle x(t), y(t)\\right\\rangle, \\qquad t\\in [a,b],\n$$\n\nwe can express the arc length of the curve in a more compact form as follows:\n\n$$\nL= \\int_{a}^{b} \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see that this is equivalent to the original arc length formula. First, note that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t) = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}, \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculating the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\| =  \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2},\n$$\n\nand thus the integral of $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$ with respect to $t$ over $[a,b]$ gives the arc length of the curve.\n\nFinally, note that we can use this arc length formula to find a curve's length in two and three dimensions." :tutorial/id #uuid "e4b94ce2-2b54-5700-81a5-6029c237a38e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2373 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "22a719d2-33dd-5817-845f-54a7f39a7c54" :step/math-academy-id 10286 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120912}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119559 :question/id #uuid "98c98a56-ca57-5dc6-9dc4-c40da53806f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3063"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc0992a-423e-5023-98d5-9d53c3b77788" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051021}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392891}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Curve"} :step/id #uuid "54797864-e2c4-5c94-af11-11469a936ca3" :step/math-academy-id 10287 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119560 :question/id #uuid "8932c22b-465f-5a48-962c-e53730e5ba40" :question/math-academy-id "e-3064"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e310cbe-9d6b-5bc4-960a-8813333bc8be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345288}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Planar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "a14aeece-5970-5d0f-b458-fcdda62e2ab6" :step/math-academy-id 10290 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120759}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121000 :tutorial/content "Given a curve $C$ parametrized by a function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, we can approximate the arc length by breaking up the curve into many small line segments and adding up their lengths.\n\nTo do this, we first choose a finite number of points $P_0,$ $P_1,$ $\\cdots,$ $P_n$ along $C$ and form the polygonal path\n\n$$\n\\overline{P_0P_1} \\cup \\overline{P_1P_2} \\cup \\cdots \\cup \\overline{P_{n-1}P_n},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d320e6a0f63a823e049203203cbc13c210e80bc98aab5a53bcb4e5299240920.png)\n\nThen, we can approximate the arc length as the sum of the lengths of the line segments:\n\n$$\nL \\approx \\, \\big\\| \\overline{P_0P_1} \\big\\| + \\big\\| \\overline{P_1P_2} \\big\\| + \\ldots + \\big\\| \\overline{P_{n-1}P_n} \\big\\|.\n$$\n\nIf $P_k$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t_k),$ then by the mean value theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{k-1}{P}_{k}}‖ & =‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖({t}_{k}-{t}_{k-1}) \\\\  & =‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖Δ{t}_{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some intermediate point $c_k \\in [t_{k-1}, t_k],$ where $\\Delta t_k = t_k - t_{k-1}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & \\approx \\,‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{0}{P}_{1}}‖+‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{1}{P}_{2}}‖+…+‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{n-1}{P}_{n}}‖ \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖Δ{t}_{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Riemann sum, and the closer the points $P_k$ are to each other, the better our approximation will be. So, in the limit as $n\\to\\infty$ (or $\\Delta t_k \\to 0),$ we have\n\n$$\nL = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}  \\| \\mathbf r'(c_k) \\| \\Delta t_k=\\int_{a}^{b} \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "236bdfbf-c5c3-5dca-9b00-44ccbf1509b5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2374 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Arc Length Formula"} :step/id #uuid "ffa01d45-b63e-5ac9-83d7-c0c1e6fb33ad" :step/math-academy-id 10293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119860 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119675 :question/id #uuid "118ad24e-627f-51ff-9b9c-9319c50956f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6303"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1889ba1a-09d4-5021-9ed2-3e531f8d5ef0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373230}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Three-Dimensional Spatial Curve"} :step/id #uuid "b6714974-ed9a-52f8-a550-bf4e4008ad1a" :step/math-academy-id 23668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120912 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121134 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate the arc length of the curve $C$ given by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)= \\left\\langle \\dfrac{t^3}{3}, \\: \\dfrac{t^2}{2}\\right\\rangle, \\qquad    t \\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe length of this curve is given by\n\n$$\nL= \\int_{0}^{1} \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|,$ we take the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ and compute its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩, \\\\ ‖{r}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{({t}^{2})^{2}+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the curve $C$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}|t|\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this integral using substitution. Let $u = t^2+1,$ then $\\textrm d u = 2 t\\,\\textrm d t.$ Therefore, our integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "615541cf-962a-50be-88eb-1d0b64de5509" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7824 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/id #uuid "3f8071cb-cf0d-5b6a-b4c1-e60ecdb69857" :step/math-academy-id 45257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120573}}] :activity/title "The Arc Length of a Vector-Valued Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120036}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120621} :activity/id #uuid "1cf102a8-b50b-50b9-b9e1-ba6d06bcd005" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048505 :topic/id #uuid "a564c0d5-78a2-53e7-86da-2f6c443f5501" :topic/math-academy-id 1770} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121018 :tutorial/content "Sometimes there are lots of calculations involved in finding the determinant of a matrix using a cofactor expansion across the first row. \n\nFortunately, however, we don't always have to use the first row! We can use *any* row we like. A cofactor expansion across any general row is known as a **Laplace expansion**.\n\nAcross the row $i,$ the Laplace expansion of an $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}{C}_{ij} \\\\  & ={a}_{i1}{C}_{i1}+{a}_{i2}{C}_{i2}+⋯+{a}_{in}{C}_{in},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}C_{ij}}$ is the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ in $A.$ Remember the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ is \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}C_{ij}}=(-1)^{i+j}{\\color{black}M_{ij}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{black}M_{ij}}$ is the minor of the entry.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the determinant of the matrix above, it is most convenient to use a Laplace expansion along the row with the most zeros. So, let's use Laplace expansion across the third row ($i=3$) as follows:\n\n![](images/6e/6e8d5f7f4111e8963a6d4c7daf2bd5116d8099e18f41c53f2dae73f659f01e35.svg)\n\nSo, we reduced a $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant." :tutorial/id #uuid "e9ee666a-da25-5521-b0bf-5d90a89b6e5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3001 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cd1eda6c-ca73-5cc1-91b6-a6fdef05cb05" :step/math-academy-id 16206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120730}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119773 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119587 :question/id #uuid "8235213e-4e88-57f6-89b0-de589ad2b38d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4418"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1637e23-6566-5e45-af4c-b27333f882d1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425353}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Row"} :step/id #uuid "c97ef0ce-8fe0-5462-8edf-ea6b6fce37f7" :step/math-academy-id 16207 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121019 :tutorial/content "We can also use the Laplace expansion across any *column* to find the determinant. Across the column $j,$ the Laplace expansion is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}{C}_{ij} \\\\  & ={a}_{1j}{C}_{1j}+{a}_{2j}{C}_{2j}+⋯+{a}_{nj}{C}_{nj},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}C_{ij}$ is the cofactor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{ij}}.$\n\nIn other words, the Laplace expansion across a row or column is the sum of the entries in the row or column multiplied by their respective cofactors.\n\nTo find the determinant of a matrix, we can choose to use the Laplace expansion across any row or column, so choose the row or column that has the most zeros, to cut down on the calculations!" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c3bdfc6-9f31-558b-9e75-725b2a92027f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3002 :tutorial/title "Laplace Expansion Across a Column"} :step/id #uuid "678f4e20-a38c-51ea-909d-5d7d1577436d" :step/math-academy-id 16208 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119588 :question/id #uuid "6f0a6499-03db-5833-b175-4835325d34d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4419"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6f57f3-d9f0-5ea7-a557-e729a8c0777f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425352}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Column"} :step/id #uuid "bb444283-a8b6-5668-8558-32d154a2839b" :step/math-academy-id 16209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120730 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119657 :question/id #uuid "5cc1443c-03cd-5f86-bcfc-b9b7f4c6a565" :question/math-academy-id "e-5182"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a88f6712-2115-55ce-abe4-b3c27e2e105f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050092}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392973}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Laplace Expansion Across a Row"} :step/id #uuid "ada1363c-ec10-55ea-a2b2-e872dcb901ad" :step/math-academy-id 19018 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120622}}] :activity/title "Finding Determinants Using Laplace Expansions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119992}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425355} :activity/id #uuid "3deab7c7-601d-5aa7-a706-f09271ec407c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048505 :topic/id #uuid "a564c0d5-78a2-53e7-86da-2f6c443f5501" :topic/math-academy-id 1770} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e448fea-5855-52bc-a7dd-48d77cb4df08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}|-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425340} {:answer/id #uuid "573033dd-3b24-588e-815d-f15a4d5a4ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}|+7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425342} {:answer/id #uuid "a302af15-3d2f-5841-a490-4042a6be47b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425345} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4a358d-1348-54aa-b944-266d98876cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425347} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7ef906-9479-54e2-b634-98c804680ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425342} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed2a715c-bbc0-55b9-9770-63cc37682a33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cd765ff8-911d-50bf-a599-8cf7e9df87e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-77807" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{14}+0\\cdot {C}_{24}+(-3)\\cdot {C}_{34}+7\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}|+7\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}|+7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0b0ff6e1-7449-568f-b593-44cc09974a71" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425356}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ac0f97-eeb8-5783-936b-1cb59033a9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336679} {:answer/id #uuid "4b236f4f-c6e5-553f-9345-4b6a8bba7c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336725} {:answer/id #uuid "6029e681-c7c2-5a8b-96eb-6ad30cbe83ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336903} {:answer/id #uuid "72576609-7b8d-50eb-8ffa-8aef7ffc7d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337056} {:answer/id #uuid "862a7584-abcb-5134-9117-8e928e486ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336679} :answer-field/id #uuid "907db199-a612-5898-a313-2594bace9c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fa886c44-6fbc-583a-a5aa-8d0e7dbb66ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-70133" :question/problem "Calculate\n$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "The $4$th row of the given matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So, using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+3\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$  determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)\\cdot {C}_{21}+2\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2-3)+2(1-3) \\\\  & =-5+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along its $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+1\\cdot {C}_{32}+1\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4-2)+(-2+1) \\\\  & =-2+(-1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-2\\cdot (-9)+3\\cdot (-3)=18-9=9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "37d0a7e2-99c5-5b88-87e2-cc62f6bed604" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf7cfbc-f2b0-5684-bbfc-df2a4dfe1cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|+7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343018} {:answer/id #uuid "6745fcba-99f1-5578-83b5-4b228c41d687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|-8\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343115} {:answer/id #uuid "96c34368-3ce8-50f2-93cc-27f082bae5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343556} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f5b999-091a-5e45-b917-5e6ca067d8b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343707} {:answer/id #uuid "bf449a53-c16e-5f60-859a-2068ef5af47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|-8\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343115} :answer-field/id #uuid "f64fee77-2b23-5772-9fa3-c06da3bf3384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "82b9ddc1-7a19-5213-af13-fb4eb5bd3ace" :question/math-academy-id "q-77808" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -7 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -8 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -7 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -8 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{12}+(-7)\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+(-8)\\cdot {C}_{42} \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|+(-8)\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|-8\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4caaadf2-991c-5e83-b9c9-f302cb53937f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425357}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "664a8be3-c96f-5f40-a756-b75cb919d17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263884} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1201de-9f59-557b-b8e0-5e3a27178624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264059} {:answer/id #uuid "a56002a4-5702-52ca-a68b-b7c638eeda99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264417} {:answer/id #uuid "e5560c16-93e7-5932-afa1-a7786605d913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264997} {:answer/id #uuid "fad808be-4742-57d4-9e6a-a4df52b11186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264997} :answer-field/id #uuid "51bf7c37-6d3e-5b7f-9709-5d6988af45d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba53d6e2-edac-5757-8fc8-0a9f2f226683" :question/math-academy-id "q-130092" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $1$st column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+-7\\cdot {C}_{21}+0\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{41} \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5c12a418-c9d4-5275-a573-e37841dac7e1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f4bae87-8e07-5dc7-9219-6f715b2ead18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425341} {:answer/id #uuid "67de8883-fc93-5260-a637-aad0f0e00eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425343} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad5be32-123d-550b-a8af-5eb97cc44261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425344} {:answer/id #uuid "a3bfe935-3937-5ddc-93c2-d9d7115d3c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425346} {:answer/id #uuid "eec09744-a423-5762-9061-2c0d2667e27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425349} :answer-field/id #uuid "836f09e9-0332-59b3-a2c7-f5f742bda069" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f2f19b4d-d8c8-5ecf-9fc4-030f5c14f82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-77803" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+4\\cdot {C}_{33}+3\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the first $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{22}+(-4)\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-4)\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(6-0)+4(0-2) \\\\  & =-12-8 \\\\  & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the second  $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+-1\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6+2)-(-6-0) \\\\  & =-4+6 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=4(-20)-3(2)=-86.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6f37f61b-9c5a-5f69-89ee-bb85a710c786" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425354}}] :activity/title "Finding Determinants Using Laplace Expansions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425339}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230044} :activity/id #uuid "e1f24137-95a9-5d07-991d-2ce3ba7ada0d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049146 :topic/id #uuid "de6ce0f8-a513-5c11-b2ac-9d3484be570b" :topic/math-academy-id 6679} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230177 :tutorial/content "Consider the first-order inhomogeneous ODE with constant coefficients given by\n\n$$\nay' + by = f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nAs we know, the method of finding the general solution is to find the sum of the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ and a particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the particular solution $y_p(x),$ we examine the functional form of the forcing term $f(x)$ and guess a form for $y_p(x)$ that matches it. \n\nAn ODE exhibits **exponential forcing** if the forcing term $f(x)$ is an exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x) = Ke^{k x},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a constant. \n\nProvided that the forcing function $e^{kx}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, our trial guess for the particular solution is an exponential function with the same exponent. That is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha\\,e^{k x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nIn this lesson, we focus on solving first-order inhomogeneous ODEs with **exponential forcing**. Let's look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "7a60ff4f-cb9d-522a-a94a-d16f43b6a04a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14449 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0804d262-6611-5c45-bc86-10e3c26e86a2" :step/math-academy-id 597439 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230227 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm{d} y} {\\textrm{d} x} + 3 y = 4 e ^ {2x}.\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm{d} y} {\\textrm{d} x} + 3 y = 0\n$$\n\nand therefore, the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c(x) =  A e^{-3x},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant. Let's now find the **particular solution** $y_p(x)$ to this equation.\n\nSince the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is the exponential function $f(x) = 4 e^{2x},$ and this is not incorporated within the complementary solution, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha e^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\alpha e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation and solve for $\\alpha{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+3(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2+3)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 5\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac{4}{5} e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the **general solution** is given by\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p = A e^{-3x} + \\dfrac{4}{5} e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can employ a similar strategy when the forcing function is a sum of exponential functions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "62cac4a5-105b-5e43-8d88-63ce73a86e94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14700 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "c3a6d6f6-44d9-519b-8417-7113691d787c" :step/math-academy-id 597440 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230046}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228896 :question/id #uuid "5e56989a-feb1-590a-8684-9442dee1b1cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21615"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305089}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "bfa02bee-ddb6-54c2-afc0-6e67786db1f4" :step/math-academy-id 597441 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230178 :tutorial/content "So far, our guesses for the particular solution have worked because the forcing term did not already appear in the complementary solution.\n\nSometimes, however, the right-hand side of the differential equation has the same form as a term that appears in the complementary solution $y_c(x).$ When this happens, our usual guess for $y_p(x)$ fails because it duplicates part of the complementary solution and cannot produce a new solution.\n\nIn this situation, we must modify our guess by *multiplying it by $x.$* Specifically, if the forcing term has the form $K e^{kx}$ and the complementary solution contains a term like $Ae^{kx},$ then instead of guessing\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha e^{kx},\n$$\n\nwe try\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\boldsymbol{x} e^{kx}.\n$$\n\nThis works because multiplying by $x$ creates a new function that is *linearly independent* of the complementary solution. As a result, our guess no longer overlaps with $y_c(x)$ and can serve as a valid particular solution. We'll discuss linear independence of functions in more detail in future lessons.\n\nLet’s see how this works in practice using a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "e6655774-c9ab-58c6-bfec-5013b08b7918" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14450 :tutorial/title "Cases When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} :step/id #uuid "b7dc2094-d676-591f-98e7-87919cdf8889" :step/math-academy-id 597445 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230229 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 10e^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 0\n$$\n\nand so the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNotice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 10 e^{-5x},$ and yet $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution. So, in this case, we assume a particular solution that takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x})^{'} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{'} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation and solve for $\\alpha.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x})+5\\alpha x{e}^{-5x} & =10{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =10{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =10{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 10x  e ^ {-5x}\n$$\n\nand thus the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x) = Ae^{-5x} + 10xe^{-5x}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c5cb7b6d-2997-5082-a597-8a0e6e4eaf5b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14703 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "11a3780c-c51f-5b21-bfc3-9b6034b2e639" :step/math-academy-id 597446 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229163 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228902 :question/id #uuid "1ea06d66-de30-514a-817f-8749abb4ab25" :question/math-academy-id "e-21621"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420166}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} :step/id #uuid "3bc4e70a-50ff-55b4-930f-052f89424e30" :step/math-academy-id 597447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229164 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228897 :question/id #uuid "a63b7c7a-2565-5330-8994-54abefbdd191" :question/math-academy-id "e-21616"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308403}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "5976dd4e-9c4c-50fb-83c5-3e9bcb60cd32" :step/math-academy-id 597451 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230051}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229165 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228898 :question/id #uuid "d2382def-85b2-5002-9614-692f272355c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21617"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363758}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "fc2e409a-3ef9-58dc-81db-f72435bd3a6c" :step/math-academy-id 597454}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs With Exponential Forcing" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229250}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420169} :activity/id #uuid "bc665116-5a76-55aa-9353-754f3071777d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049146 :topic/id #uuid "de6ce0f8-a513-5c11-b2ac-9d3484be570b" :topic/math-academy-id 6679} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420167 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87aacf8e-bc15-5baf-95e2-dc84ee4735c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{e}^{7x}+5x{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306441} {:answer/id #uuid "a6180aa2-764b-5335-9218-b9ecd269a77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(5x+A)e^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420162} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef304257-c02b-5b9a-91de-da2bbd172314" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cafd04c6-8f34-538e-bfbf-4314bcafa2be" :question/math-academy-id "q-333168" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-7y=5{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{7x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{7x},$ and ${e}^{7x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{7x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{7x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{7x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{7x}+7x{e}^{7x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+7x){e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+7x){e}^{7x})-7\\alpha x{e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ (1+7x-7x)\\alpha {e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7x}+5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c91bcfc-99f4-5bfe-804e-7364e7854038"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420168 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6d8db23-4013-5d8a-8bb0-41b6f6fa3c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420163}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420163} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7b7917-2ac5-5b52-a8e9-071c6e798cfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "036131be-8602-586a-8ee5-fe7271608cce" :question/math-academy-id "q-332723" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=6{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2\\alpha x{e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "748c2caa-2098-5b2d-8ce4-acf93b07faac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420170}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420169 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b3766c-24fe-5e39-af92-4bf51a3a4723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9xe^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420164} :answer-field/id #uuid "f31cbcf7-0ca7-5dff-a5ff-a27c6b8e3c33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "388312be-28d6-5dd3-90fa-f6561f195420" :question/math-academy-id "q-332708" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=9{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{-6x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-6x}-6x{e}^{-6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x})+6\\alpha x{e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ (1-6x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=9x{e}^{-6x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7a3db07d-09b8-53c0-a769-ee5a0527289c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420168}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420170 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0041758a-5182-50fb-a219-5d3f3ef02024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305153} {:answer/id #uuid "55f5feee-d649-5b39-a104-bdb383b1096c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8x-1)e^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420161} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d58e7d-e179-5799-97c4-0c9c22e0553a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d63971f5-c416-5771-8c44-552fcfc50a79" :question/math-academy-id "q-332936" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x})+2(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}) & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (1-2x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "adfad238-f91c-58f5-8dc5-b475c8a368d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420171}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420171 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca491ec8-db7c-5b57-b504-cd04e9792609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307030} {:answer/id #uuid "3e468feb-d4ae-5e37-bcbd-d076dac5bb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{5x}+15xe^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420160} :answer-field/id #uuid "416fb058-4b84-52e5-8668-5c9c862047fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ceb57bf0-d702-511f-a8e6-4fe4eee1bf79" :question/math-academy-id "q-332938" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=15{e}^{5x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=15{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}) & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c64f98e2-0858-55ea-8d34-04c204a10d14" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420167}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs With Exponential Forcing" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420159}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106837} :activity/id #uuid "6d587675-28ef-5040-83fe-9aed98369605" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638 :topic/id #uuid "0748b9c2-fae7-5522-9655-db9e6f1d50c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1347} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107140 :tutorial/content "Recall that a random variable $X$ is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A random variable can take on one of many values from a set $S,$ called the range or support of $X.$\n\nA **continuous random variable** is a random variable whose set of possible values $S$ is given by an interval of real numbers.\n\nFor example, the height of a randomly selected person could be modeled as a continuous random variable because its possible values form an interval of real numbers. For modeling purposes, we usually assume that the height of a randomly selected person could be *any number* in the real interval $(0,\\infty),$ although we could also restrict this to a finite interval $[a,b]$ if we wanted.\n\nOn the other hand, the result of rolling a $6$-sided die is *not* a continuous random variable because its possible values are $1,2,3,4,5,6,$ which does *not* correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nPhysical quantities, such as mass, length, area, volume, time, fluid concentration, etc, are usually modeled as continuous variables. \n\n![](images/73/73d1a2c3852734a6221654882ad5421adb565c96c718e91283e5fbc1f26a72c7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "56f14280-d510-530e-9f12-5e21e8dab7e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6769 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8a357dbd-8538-5f7a-a808-d0bb39634099" :step/math-academy-id 41069 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105860 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105444 :question/id #uuid "e7c527c9-2dc1-50b2-bbf6-10c1a9d122dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10837"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369331}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"} :step/id #uuid "6786cf48-e665-5cb3-ba1f-c245b903bc5f" :step/math-academy-id 41070 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107141 :tutorial/content "The **probability density function** (or **pdf**) of a continuous random variable $X$ defined over a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ such that the probability that $X$ lies in the interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nThus, the probability that $X$ lies between $a$ and $b$ can be interpreted as the area bounded between $f(x)$ and the $x$-axis over the interval $x\\in [a,b],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6c21e69a0dbd15ce32d18c2e74d8fcab0276e82bc55b354eaa5140adff610e6.png)\n\nIn general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function over a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor example, let the random variable $X$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $6.$ In this case, the pdf of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis pdf tells us that any number between $0$ and $6$ has an equal chance of being selected, and no number outside this interval can be selected. \n\nNote that this is a valid pdf because it's always nonnegative, and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}x]}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability that our randomly selected number is no greater than $3,$ we integrate the pdf over the interval $[0,3]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}x]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 3]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result matches our intuition. Since every number has an equal chance of being selected, and $[0,3]$ is half the length of $[0,6],$ we must have $P(0 \\leq X \\leq 3) = \\dfrac12.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "aeeb80c6-8faa-5653-8729-e6dc2c52a2e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6770 :tutorial/title "Probability Density Functions"} :step/id #uuid "97ac3c7c-a2f7-585b-a8c6-99d14604c5d2" :step/math-academy-id 41073 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107142 :tutorial/content "The probability density function of a continuous random variable $X$ is somewhat analogous to the probability *mass* function of a discrete random variable in the sense that it describes the probability distribution of $X.$ \n\nHowever, unlike the case with discrete random variables, probability density is *not* the same as probability! Instead, we can think of a pdf as a tool for computing probabilities for continuous random variables.  \n\nThere are two counterintuitive yet important points to be aware of when it comes to interpreting a pdf:\n\n- *The probability that a continuous random variable takes on any particular value is always equal to $0.$* \n\nContinuing the previous example, the probability that our random variable takes on the value $X=5$ is equal to\n\n$$\nP(X = 5) = P(5 \\leq X \\leq 5) = \\int_5^5 \\dfrac{1}{6} \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nIn general, for any value $a$ and any continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X = a) = P(a \\leq X \\leq a) = \\int_a^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- *The values of a probability density function may be greater than $1.$* \n\nFor example, let $Y$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ Then, the pdf of $Y$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(y) = 2, \\qquad y \\in \\left[ 0, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right].\n$$\n\nThis pdf tells us that all numbers between $0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ are equally likely. Again, $f(y)$ is nonnegative, and \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1/2} f(y) \\, \\textrm d y = \\int_0^{1/2} 2 \\, \\textrm d y = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(y)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nLastly, note that since the probability that a continuous random variable takes on a particular value is always equal to $0,$ it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of a particular interval. In other words, for any continuous random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X < b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3bac600c-991c-55c0-8e73-72e609d149f5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6771 :tutorial/title "Interpreting Probability Density Functions"} :step/id #uuid "e61652ca-95a2-5028-b7e7-e01a1b6fa9b4" :step/math-academy-id 41074 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105445 :question/id #uuid "295d2025-c958-55e3-a194-5cdb271a510c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10838"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376075}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"} :step/id #uuid "39c447c4-4037-52b1-ab5b-5e15228f5477" :step/math-academy-id 41075 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105454 :question/id #uuid "32131e34-3141-51b3-8d26-6cee481f168c" :question/math-academy-id "e-11037"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369866}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"} :step/id #uuid "50dd0bf2-ab05-5ddb-8a4b-93e4fad0d2e1" :step/math-academy-id 41931}] :activity/title "Probability Density Functions of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105985}]}
